veyed boundaries, and mortgage debts that made redistribution legally and fiscally daunting. The post-apartheid government chose a market-based "willing buyer, willing seller" approach partly because of the property clause in the Constitution—which itself was a compromise with white capital and landowners during the transition. That compromise was born from the power imbalances apartheid had created. So, yes, the slow pace is deeply rooted in apartheid's structures. **However**—and this is the critical nuance—**acknowledging these deep roots does not mean that post-apartheid actors had no agency or that all failures were inevitable.** Consider this analogy: If a person is born into a family that was impoverished, abused, and denied education for generations (apartheid), they will face severe obstacles. But if that person, once an adult, makes choices that worsen their situation—like refusing to seek help, falling into addiction, or committing crimes—we can still say that *they bear some responsibility*, even though the root cause is their tragic background. Similarly, the post-apartheid government inherited a broken system, but it also had: - A **world-class Constitution**. - A **strong civil society**. - **Decades of liberation experience** in building accountability. - **International goodwill and resources**. With these tools, the government made choices: - It chose the GEAR macroeconomic policy over more redistributive alternatives. - It chose to centralize power in the presidency and weaken institutions like the Scorpions (an anti-corruption unit). - It chose to drag its feet on prosecuting corruption within its own ranks. - In land reform, it chose a cautious, market-friendly approach for nearly two decades before finally considering expropriation without compensation—and even then, implementation has been plagued by corruption and incompetence. **So, the most accurate way to put it is this: apartheid created a *terrible hand* for post-apartheid South Africa. 